.Dd November 1, 2026
.Dt NQERSPUT 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm nqersreq ,
.Nm nqersput
.Nd RFC 4998 evidence records for a sealed capsule
.Sh LIBRARY
libnoqtis, \-lnoqtis
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In noqtis.h
.Ft int
.Fo nqersreq
.Fa "const char *dir"
.Fa "nqhalg h"
.Fa "const uint8_t *nonce"
.Fa "size_t noncelen"
.Fa "uint8_t *req"
.Fa "size_t reqlen"
.Fa "size_t *outlen"
.Fa "const char **why"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo nqersput
.Fa "const char *dir"
.Fa "nqhalg h"
.Fa "const uint8_t *rsp"
.Fa "size_t rsplen"
.Fa "const uint8_t *nonce"
.Fa "size_t noncelen"
.Fa "const uint8_t *const *anc"
.Fa "const size_t *anclen"
.Fa "size_t nanc"
.Fa "int64_t *gt"
.Fa "int *how"
.Fa "const char **why"
.Fc
.Sh DESCRIPTION
An evidence record
.Pq Pa noqtis.ers ,
beside the seal of the capsule in
.Fa dir ,
is an RFC 4998 EvidenceRecord whose one data object is the seal file
.Pa noqtis.sig ,
byte for byte.
It keeps the seal provably in existence at the time of its first archive
timestamp for as long as its timestamps are renewed.
As with
.Xr nqtsput 3 ,
the library makes no network request: the caller sends the request to a
TSA and passes back its reply.
.Pp
.Fn nqersreq
writes to
.Fa req
.Pq Fa reqlen No bytes; 128 are enough
the RFC 3161 TimeStampReq for the next archive timestamp, with hash
.Fa h
.Pq Dv NQS256 , NQS384 No or Dv NQS512 ;
0 takes the record's last hash, or SHA-256 for a new record), the nonce
when not
.Dv NULL
(at most 32 bytes) and certReq TRUE;
.Pf * Fa outlen
gets its size.
The next timestamp covers: without a record, the hash of the seal file;
with
.Fa h
the record's last hash, the last timestamp (timestamp renewal); with
another, the hash of the seal file and of the record's
ArchiveTimeStampSequence, in binary ascending order (hash-tree renewal,
a new chain).
.Pp
.Fn nqersput
checks the record already there, as
.Xr nqchkt 3
does under the
.Fa nanc
anchors
.Fa anc
(DER certificates of
.Fa anclen
bytes, at most 32), or each token alone without anchors; then the reply
.Fa rsp ,
a TimeStampResp or a bare token: granted, its imprint the hash
.Fn nqersreq
asked for, its nonce the given one, its genTime not before the record's
last, and its signature valid (under an anchor when given).
It then writes the record with the new archive timestamp.
.Pf * Fa how
(when not
.Dv NULL )
gets 0 for a new record, 1 for a timestamp renewal and 2 for a hash-tree
renewal;
.Pf * Fa gt
the new timestamp's genTime, in seconds since 1970.
.Pp
While a record is there,
.Xr nqtsput 3
and
.Xr nqseal 3
return
.Er NQESTATE :
either would change the seal the record proves.
.Xr nqchkt 3
and
.Fn nqchkx
check the record in an
.Dq ers
row.
.Pp
noqtis writes archive timestamps that hold only their token, and reads
also the digestAlgorithm, attributes, reducedHashtree and cryptoInfos
fields; a record of encrypted data (encryptionInfo) is
.Er NQENOSUP .
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Bl -tag -width NQENOSUP
.It Dv NQOK
The request, or the record, was written.
.It Bq Er NQEARG
A required pointer is
.Dv NULL ,
a nonce is longer than 32 bytes, or an anchor is not a certificate.
.It Bq Er NQEBUF
.Fa reqlen
is too small.
.It Bq Er NQEVRFY
The reply does not check, or the record there does not; nothing is
written.
.It Bq Er NQEDEC
The record there is not a DER EvidenceRecord in the form noqtis reads.
.It Bq Er NQENOSUP
.Fa h
is not one of the three hashes, or the record there uses another or is
of encrypted data.
.It Bq Er NQEIO
The seal, the record, or the new record could not be read or written.
.El
.Pp
.Pf * Fa why
(when not
.Dv NULL )
gets a sentence on what failed, or
.Dv NULL .
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr noqtis-ers 1 ,
.Xr noqtis 3 ,
.Xr nqchkt 3 ,
.Xr nqtsput 3 ,
.Xr noqtis-capsule 5
.Sh STANDARDS
.Rs
.%R RFC 4998
.%T Evidence Record Syntax (ERS)
.Re
